The practical test is contrast: **dark text on a light background, or light on dark, and check it at arm''s length on a phone.** If you have to lean in, the pairing has failed regardless of how good it looks on your laptop. Two saturated complementary colours directly against each other vibrate unpleasantly and are almost never right for text.

**One addition from me, since no video covers it well:** colour *meaning* is far more culturally specific than most design advice admits. Associations that hold in one market do not travel — white means purity in some places and mourning in others, and red carries very different weight across contexts. Know your actual audience rather than trusting a chart.',
      'Take a headline and set it in three colour pairings — complementary, analogous, and dark-on-light. View each on your phone at arm''s length and note which you can still read comfortably.',
