      'Also tool-agnostic. Type is the single fastest way to make work look amateur or professional, and the difference is mostly restraint.

The vocabulary is worth learning properly because it turns "this looks off" into a fixable observation: **leading** is the space between lines, **tracking** the spacing across a run of text, **kerning** the space between two specific letters. Loose leading in body text is one of the cheapest readability wins available.

On pairing: **two fonts is plenty**, and the reliable trick is contrast in role, not similarity — a distinctive display font for headings, a plain workhorse for body. Two fonts that are nearly alike read as a mistake rather than a choice. And the fonts to avoid are avoidable for a reason: they carry so much cultural baggage that the reader notices the font instead of the message.',
      'Set a poster with one display font for the heading and one plain font for the body. Then increase the body leading by half a line and compare readability. Then try it with two very similar fonts and see how muddled it looks.',
